Dacquoise and praline buttercream
6
Almond dacquoise filled with hazelnut praline buttercream.

---
Dacquoise
egg whites|250|g
powdered sugar|100|g
almond meal|200|g
powdered sugar|150|g
fine salt|1|g

Praline buttercream
whole eggs|60|g
egg yolks|50|g
powdered sugar|200|g
water|10|ml
soft butter|200|g
hazelnut praline paste|50|g

Finish
powdered sugar|10|g

---
Make the dacquoise: sift the powdered sugar.
Mix the almond meal and powdered sugar.
Beat the egg whites to stiff peaks with the salt (optional).
Add the sugar in three additions to get a firm meringue.
Gently fold in the almond–sugar mixture in two goes with a spatula.
Spread on a baking-paper-lined tray about 1 cm thick.
Smooth and trim to a neat rectangle.
Bake at 190°C about 8 min.
Cool on a rack then cut two discs or rectangles to the desired size (e.g. 2 × 18 cm discs).

Make the praline buttercream: take the butter out in advance so it is soft.
Cook the sugar with a little water to 117–120°C.
While the syrup cooks, whisk the whole eggs and yolks.
Pour the syrup in a thin stream onto the eggs with the mixer on low, then increase speed.
Whisk until you have a thick, creamy sabayon at room temperature.
Add the butter in small cubes, bit by bit, whisking until smooth.
Fold in the hazelnut praline and keep in a piping bag in the fridge.

Assembly: pipe blobs of buttercream onto the first dacquoise disc.
Top with the second disc.
Dust lightly with powdered sugar.

---
Salt in the egg whites is optional but can help them hold.
Dacquoise can also be made with hazelnut meal.
Make sure butter and sabayon are at the same temperature so the cream does not split.

